cleaning this Park in Greater Buenos  Aires is the only way these women have  of making a living they're part of a  social movement that helps poor people  around the country find a job we're a  cooperative that provides a service to  the municipality we started 12 years ago  it's the only job I've found they're  paid by the Argentine government around  130 dollars a month some people call us  lazy because we depend on the government  but we work and we provide a service we  also do courses and go to school for the  past several weeks the center of owner  situs has been repeatedly paralyzed by  organizations demanding more government  help the devaluation of the peso  currency has had a strong impact on the  price of food and social movement say  they do not have enough social movements  were born after the economic crisis in  2001 and they stayed and they play a  role in keeping the streets active they  copy how labor unions operate in  Argentina and they are an example of  what happens when politics reaches the  streets emilio Persico is the leader of  the Evita movement named after the wife  of Argentina's former president Juan  Peron he says his organization is  playing a crucial role in preventing the  situation in the country from going out  of control social movements are part of  the solution because we work where the  state does not some people want to  demonize us because we block a road but  that's not everything we do we are the  ones preventing people from running to a  supermarket and looting le de cachoeira  is part of the Evita movement  she says she worked all her life even  though she lived in a slum she's been  able to buy a home but now she can  hardly pay the bills I have to pay  electricity and gas and we can barely  buy food I have worked all my life and  what's happening to us is humiliating  since taking office Mauricio Macri  eliminated the subsidy on utility  services in an attempt to regularize an  economy that has been in the red for  years people here tell us that the  economic crisis in Argentina has an  enormous impact  in their lives they also say that thanks  to social movements they at least have a  job the problem is that in the past they  used to be able to fill the fridge with  food while now it's almost empty and  that's why many are on the streets these  days demanding the government does not  look away Teresa Waugh al Jazeera Buenos  Aires
 

